# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# Local environment
.env

# Harness logs (ephemeral, regenerated)
.logs/

# Local /wayfinder tracker
.scratch/

# Per-machine Claude Code state. The skills and agents ARE committed - see below.
.claude/settings.local.json

# `.mcp.json` stays out, and this one is not a judgement call: it hardcodes absolute
# paths from the machine it was generated on (`/home/<user>/.nvm/.../npx`) plus a
# `framework` binary this repository no longer depends on. It would not work anywhere
# else, so committing it would be committing something broken.
.mcp.json

# `.claude/skills/` and `.claude/agents/` used to be ignored here, with the reason
# "third-party content, not this project's work". That was wrong twice over. Half of
# them are this workflow's own - the NDJSON logging contract, the LOGS-READ gate, the
# Context7 rule, the visual-evidence contract - and README's "AI in My Workflow" and
# ARCHITECTURE §7b both describe them as the gates the work passed through. A document
# that describes a workflow whose artifacts are absent from the repository is asking to
# be taken on trust, which is the one thing this submission tries never to do.
#
# They carry no absolute paths and no secrets; ~390K of markdown. `.dockerignore` keeps
# them out of the image, which is where "development tooling never ships" is enforced.
